"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} to §{#}. Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Where it is in the demo:</strong> the Optics Bench, with the Concave mirror, Refracting surface and Apparent depth setups.",
"<strong>Using the demo.</strong> An object arrow sends a fan of rays toward a mirror or a refracting surface. Click an element to open its editor: a mirror can be concave, convex or plane with a radius \\(R\\); a refracting surface has indices \\(n_1\\) (object side) and \\(n_2\\) (far side), a radius and an aperture. The object’s editor sets its height and the half angle and aim of its fan of rays. Drag the object to change its distance. The Image section gives the object distance, the image distance and magnification found from the traced rays, and the image’s kind; it uses the signs below.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> Every ray obeys the law of reflection or Snell’s law exactly, at the true spherical (or flat) surface. The image is where the traced rays from the tip of the arrow cross (a least squares fit), and the magnification compares the image of the tip with the image of the base. So the demo shows the real behavior of spherical surfaces, including the blur of rays far from the axis, and the mirror and surface equations below hold only for rays near the axis. Simulated data.",
"Find where mirrors and curved glass surfaces form images, test the mirror equation and the equation for a refracting surface, and see where these equations stop working.",
"A plane mirror forms a virtual image as far behind the mirror as the object is in front of it, upright and the same size.",
"A spherical mirror of radius \\(R\\) has focal length \\(f = R/2\\). For rays close to the axis, an object a distance \\(d_o\\) in front of the mirror has its image at \\(d_i\\), with",
"\\[\\frac{1}{d_o} + \\frac{1}{d_i} = \\frac{1}{f}, \\qquad m = -\\frac{d_i}{d_o}.\\]",
"Signs (OpenStax): \\(d_o > 0\\) for a real object; \\(d_i > 0\\) for a real image, in front of the mirror, and \\(d_i < 0\\) for a virtual image behind it; \\(f\\) and \\(R\\) are positive for a concave mirror and negative for a convex one; \\(m < 0\\) means the image is inverted.",
"A single spherical surface of radius \\(R\\) between media of indices \\(n_1\\) (the object’s side) and \\(n_2\\) forms an image with",
"\\[\\frac{n_1}{d_o} + \\frac{n_2}{d_i} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}, \\qquad m = -\\frac{n_1 d_i}{n_2 d_o},\\]",
"where \\(R > 0\\) when the center of curvature is on the far side, and \\(d_i > 0\\) for a real image on the far side. For a flat surface, \\(R \\to \\infty\\) and \\(d_i = -(n_2/n_1)\\,d_o\\): an object under water looks closer to the surface than it is. This is apparent depth.",
"All of these equations are paraxial: they assume every ray stays close to the axis and makes a small angle with it.",
